{
  "result": "I have always loved the hour before the library closes, when the light turns amber and the dust becomes visible. People speak more quietly then, as if they are already apologizing to the empty chairs they will leave behind. Outside, the street performs its ordinary miracles: buses sighing, bicycles ringing, a baker locking his door with flour still on his sleeves. I stay because the quiet is not empty; it is crowded with unfinished thoughts. Every book on the nearest shelf seems to be breathing slowly, waiting for someone to misread it beautifully. When I was twelve, I believed libraries stored thunder, and that librarians were merely translators employed by the weather. Now I think they store something stranger: the versions of ourselves we almost became on particular afternoons. There is the self who finished the novel, the self who wrote the letter, the self who finally learned to swim. They all linger between pages, patient and slightly annoyed, asking why we keep choosing the familiar exit. Tonight I am reading about deep sea fish that invent their own light because the sun has given up on them. It seems like honest work, carrying a small lamp through enormous darkness without demanding the darkness explain itself first. Perhaps that is all any of us are doing, inventing local light: kindness, jokes, soup, remembered birthdays, correctly pronounced names. The librarian taps her desk, not unkindly, and the spell loosens just enough for coats and bags and goodbyes. I leave, carrying light.",
